/* Original, concise revision notes. Verify examinable topics against CBSE 2026–27. */
window.STUDY_NOTES = {
"Mathematics": {
"Real Numbers": {overview:"Use prime factorisation to understand HCF, LCM and irrational numbers. For two positive integers a and b, HCF(a,b) × LCM(a,b) = a × b.",points:["Fundamental theorem of arithmetic: every composite number has a unique prime factorisation, apart from the order of factors.","For a rational number p/q in lowest terms, its decimal expansion terminates exactly when q has no prime factors other than 2 and 5.","To prove √p irrational for prime p, assume it is a/b in lowest terms and use divisibility to reach a contradiction."],practice:"Find HCF and LCM using prime factorisation. Decide whether 13/3125 has a terminating decimal expansion."},
"Polynomials": {overview:"A polynomial is an expression in a variable with non-negative integer powers. Zeros are values that make the polynomial equal to zero.",points:["For ax² + bx + c, if zeroes are α and β, then α + β = −b/a and αβ = c/a.","A polynomial graph meets the x-axis at its real zeroes.","Use the relationship between zeroes and coefficients to build or verify a quadratic polynomial."],practice:"Find the zeroes of x² − 5x + 6 and verify their sum and product."},
"Pair of Linear Equations in Two Variables": {overview:"Two linear equations represent two lines. Their intersection determines the solution.",points:["For a₁x+b₁y+c₁=0 and a₂x+b₂y+c₂=0, a unique solution occurs when a₁/a₂ ≠ b₁/b₂.","If a₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ ≠ c₁/c₂, the lines are parallel and inconsistent.","If all three ratios are equal, the equations represent the same line and have infinitely many solutions. Solve by substitution, elimination or graphing."],practice:"Solve 2x+y=7 and x−y=2. Interpret a pair of equations with no solution."},
"Quadratic Equations": {overview:"A quadratic equation has the form ax² + bx + c = 0, where a ≠ 0.",points:["Factorise when possible; otherwise use x = (−b ± √(b²−4ac))/(2a).","The discriminant D=b²−4ac determines the roots: D>0 distinct real roots; D=0 equal real roots; D<0 no real roots.","Convert word problems into equations and reject roots that do not fit the context."],practice:"Solve 2x² − 7x + 3 = 0 and identify the discriminant."},
"Arithmetic Progressions": {overview:"An arithmetic progression (AP) has a constant difference d between consecutive terms.",points:["nth term: aₙ = a + (n−1)d.","Sum of first n terms: Sₙ = n/2[2a+(n−1)d] = n/2(a+l), where l is the last term.","Check whether a sequence is an AP by subtracting consecutive terms."],practice:"Find the 20th term of 3, 7, 11, … and the sum of its first 20 terms."},
"Triangles": {overview:"Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles and proportional corresponding sides.",points:["Basic Proportionality Theorem: a line parallel to one side of a triangle divides the other two sides proportionally.","Criteria for similarity: AA, SAS and SSS.","For similar triangles, ratio of areas equals the square of the ratio of corresponding sides. Apply Pythagoras in right triangles."],practice:"Two similar triangles have corresponding sides in ratio 3:5. Find their area ratio."},
"Coordinate Geometry": {overview:"Coordinates locate points on the Cartesian plane and allow distances, midpoints and section ratios to be calculated.",points:["Distance between (x₁,y₁) and (x₂,y₂): √[(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²].","Midpoint: ((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2).","A point dividing a segment internally in ratio m:n has coordinates ((mx₂+nx₁)/(m+n), (my₂+ny₁)/(m+n))."],practice:"Find the distance between (2,−1) and (−4,7)."},
"Introduction to Trigonometry": {overview:"Trigonometric ratios relate angles to sides in a right triangle.",points:["sin θ=opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ=adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ=opposite/adjacent.","Reciprocal ratios: cosec θ=1/sin θ, sec θ=1/cos θ, cot θ=1/tan θ.","Identities: sin²θ+cos²θ=1; 1+tan²θ=sec²θ; 1+cot²θ=cosec²θ. Memorise standard values at 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°."],practice:"Evaluate sin 30° + cos 60° and simplify (1−sin²θ)/cos²θ."},
"Some Applications of Trigonometry": {overview:"Heights and distances use angles of elevation and depression with right-triangle trigonometry.",points:["Draw and label a diagram before selecting a ratio.","Angle of elevation is measured upward from the horizontal; angle of depression downward.","Choose tan θ=height/base when opposite and adjacent sides are involved. Keep units consistent."],practice:"From a point 20 m from a tower, its angle of elevation is 45°. Estimate the tower height if eye height is ignored."},
"Circles": {overview:"A tangent touches a circle at exactly one point.",points:["The tangent at a point is perpendicular to the radius through the point of contact.","Tangents drawn from a common external point to a circle are equal in length.","Use right triangles formed by radii and tangents to solve length problems."],practice:"A circle has radius 5 cm and an external point is 13 cm from its centre. Find the tangent length."},
"Areas Related to Circles": {overview:"Use circle, sector and segment formulas with the central angle.",points:["Circumference = 2πr; area = πr².","Sector area = (θ/360°)πr²; arc length = (θ/360°)2πr.","Segment area is the sector area minus the area of the triangle formed by the two radii and chord. Identify shaded regions carefully."],practice:"Find the area of a 90° sector of a circle of radius 14 cm using π=22/7."},
"Surface Areas and Volumes": {overview:"Separate combined solids into familiar shapes and account for exposed surfaces only.",points:["Cylinder: curved surface 2πrh, total surface 2πr(h+r), volume πr²h.","Cone: slant height l=√(r²+h²), curved surface πrl, volume πr²h/3.","Sphere: surface 4πr², volume 4πr³/3; hemisphere volume 2πr³/3. Use consistent cubic units for volume."],practice:"Find the volume of a cylinder with radius 3 cm and height 10 cm."},
"Statistics": {overview:"Statistics summarises grouped data using mean, median and mode.",points:["Mean for grouped data: Σfᵢxᵢ/Σfᵢ, where xᵢ is the class mark.","Median = l + [(N/2 − cf)/f]h, where l is lower boundary of the median class.","Mode = l + [(f₁−f₀)/(2f₁−f₀−f₂)]h. Identify the correct class and values before substituting."],practice:"Find class marks for intervals 10–20, 20–30 and 30–40."},
"Probability": {overview:"For equally likely outcomes, probability measures how likely an event is.",points:["P(E)=number of favourable outcomes / total equally likely outcomes.","0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1; P(not E)=1−P(E).","List outcomes systematically to avoid missing or double-counting possibilities."],practice:"A fair die is thrown once. Find the probability of getting a prime number."}
},
"Science": {
"Chemical Reactions and Equations": {overview:"Chemical reactions rearrange atoms to form new substances. Equations must be balanced to obey conservation of mass.",points:["Balance equations by changing coefficients, never subscripts.","Types include combination, decomposition, displacement, double displacement, oxidation and reduction.","Observe gas, colour, precipitate, temperature or state changes as evidence. Oxidation involves gain of oxygen or loss of hydrogen in common examples."],practice:"Balance Fe + H₂O → Fe₃O₄ + H₂ and classify the reaction."},
"Acids, Bases and Salts": {overview:"Acids and bases change indicator colours and react with each other in neutralisation.",points:["Acids produce H⁺/H₃O⁺ ions in water; bases produce OH⁻ ions in aqueous solution.","Acid + base → salt + water. Acids reacting with many metals release hydrogen; carbonates release CO₂ with acids.","pH below 7 is acidic, 7 neutral and above 7 basic at 25°C. Learn uses and preparation of common salts such as baking soda, washing soda, bleaching powder and plaster of Paris."],practice:"What gas is released when dilute hydrochloric acid reacts with sodium carbonate? How can it be tested?"},
"Metals and Non-metals": {overview:"Physical and chemical properties help distinguish metals from non-metals, with important exceptions.",points:["Metals are generally malleable, ductile, lustrous and good conductors; non-metals are generally brittle and poor conductors.","Reactivity series predicts displacement. More reactive metals displace less reactive metals from solutions of their salts.","Ionic compounds form through electron transfer; they generally have high melting points and conduct electricity when molten or dissolved, not as solids."],practice:"Why can copper displace silver from silver nitrate solution but not zinc from zinc sulphate?"},
"Carbon and its Compounds": {overview:"Carbon forms covalent compounds because of its tetravalency and ability to make chains and rings.",points:["Covalent bonds share electron pairs. Carbon commonly forms single, double and triple bonds.","Saturated hydrocarbons have single bonds; unsaturated hydrocarbons contain double or triple bonds. Learn homologous series and functional groups.","Ethanol reacts with sodium and oxidises; ethanoic acid reacts with carbonates and bases. Soap forms micelles and cleans greasy dirt; detergents work better in hard water."],practice:"Distinguish saturated and unsaturated hydrocarbons with one example each."},
"Life Processes": {overview:"Living organisms maintain themselves through nutrition, respiration, transport and excretion.",points:["Autotrophic nutrition in plants uses chlorophyll, light, CO₂ and water to make food. Human digestion involves enzymes and absorption in the small intestine.","Aerobic respiration releases more energy than anaerobic respiration. Gas exchange occurs in lungs through alveoli.","Plants transport water through xylem and food through phloem. Human blood, heart and vessels transport materials; kidneys filter blood to form urine."],practice:"Explain how alveoli are adapted for gas exchange."},
"Control and Coordination": {overview:"Nervous and hormonal systems coordinate responses and maintain internal balance.",points:["A neuron receives and transmits impulses; reflex arcs provide rapid responses through the spinal cord.","Plant hormones include auxin, gibberellins, cytokinins and abscisic acid. Tropic movements respond to directional stimuli.","Endocrine glands release hormones into blood. Insulin regulates blood glucose; adrenaline prepares the body for emergency action."],practice:"Draw a labelled reflex arc and describe its sequence."},
"How do Organisms Reproduce?": {overview:"Reproduction produces new individuals and passes genetic information to the next generation.",points:["Asexual reproduction uses one parent and includes fission, budding, fragmentation, regeneration and vegetative propagation.","Sexual reproduction involves gamete formation and fertilisation. Flowering plants use pollination followed by fertilisation and seed formation.","Human reproductive systems produce gametes; contraception helps prevent pregnancy, and barrier methods also reduce transmission of many sexually transmitted infections."],practice:"Differentiate pollination from fertilisation."},
"Heredity": {overview:"Heredity passes traits from parents to offspring through genes; variations arise from genetic recombination and other causes.",points:["Genes are DNA segments on chromosomes. Alternative forms of a gene are alleles.","In a simple monohybrid cross, a dominant allele can mask a recessive allele in a heterozygote. Genotype and phenotype are different concepts.","In humans, XX is typically associated with female sex and XY with male sex; the sperm contributes either X or Y. Traits are often influenced by many genes and environment."],practice:"Cross Tt × Tt and find expected genotype and phenotype ratios for complete dominance."},
"Light: Reflection and Refraction": {overview:"Ray diagrams and sign conventions help explain image formation by mirrors and lenses.",points:["Mirror formula: 1/f=1/v+1/u; magnification m=−v/u. Follow the Cartesian sign convention consistently.","Refraction is the change in direction of light when it passes between media; refractive index relates the speed of light in vacuum to that in a medium.","Lens formula: 1/f=1/v−1/u; magnification m=v/u. Learn principal focus, optical centre, ray rules and power P=1/f in metres."],practice:"A convex lens has focal length +20 cm. Find its power in dioptres."},
"The Human Eye and the Colourful World": {overview:"The eye focuses light on the retina and adjusts focus for nearby and distant objects.",points:["Accommodation changes focal length through the ciliary muscles. The near point of a normal adult eye is approximately 25 cm.","Myopia is corrected by a concave lens; hypermetropia by a convex lens; presbyopia may require suitable converging or bifocal lenses.","Dispersion splits white light into colours. Atmospheric refraction explains twinkling of stars; scattering contributes to the blue sky and reddish sunrise/sunset."],practice:"Which lens corrects myopia and why?"},
"Electricity": {overview:"Current, potential difference and resistance describe electric circuits.",points:["Ohm’s law: V=IR when physical conditions remain constant. Resistance depends on material, length, cross-sectional area and temperature.","Series resistances add; in parallel, 1/Rp=1/R₁+1/R₂+…. In series current is common; in parallel potential difference is common.","Power P=VI=I²R=V²/R. Electrical energy E=Pt; commercial energy is measured in kWh. Convert units carefully."],practice:"A 6 Ω resistor carries 2 A. Find voltage and power."},
"Magnetic Effects of Electric Current": {overview:"Electric currents create magnetic fields; magnets and currents exert forces in suitable conditions.",points:["Magnetic field lines go from north to south outside a magnet and never cross. Field around a straight conductor forms concentric circles.","Right-hand thumb rule gives field direction around a current-carrying conductor; Fleming’s left-hand rule gives force direction on a current-carrying conductor.","A motor converts electrical energy to mechanical energy. Electromagnetic induction produces current from changing magnetic fields; household circuits use live, neutral and earth wires for safety."],practice:"State Fleming’s left-hand rule and one use of an electric motor."},
"Our Environment": {overview:"Ecosystems contain living organisms, non-living components and flows of energy and matter.",points:["Producers make food; consumers feed on other organisms; decomposers recycle nutrients.","Energy flows through trophic levels and decreases at each transfer. The 10% law is a simplified model of energy transfer.","Biodegradable substances can be broken down by biological processes; non-biodegradable pollutants may persist and bioaccumulate. Ozone protects life from harmful ultraviolet radiation."],practice:"Why are food chains usually short? Explain biomagnification."}
},
"Social Science": {
"The Rise of Nationalism in Europe": {overview:"Nationalism reshaped Europe through revolutions, cultural movements, unification and changing political boundaries.",points:["The French Revolution promoted ideas of citizenship and a nation based on the people; Napoleon spread some reforms while imposing control.","Liberal nationalism supported equality before law and constitutional government, but voting rights were often limited. Romanticism helped create shared cultural identity.","Germany unified under Prussian leadership in 1871; Italy unified through several leaders and stages. The Balkans became a source of tension among national groups and empires."],practice:"Explain how culture contributed to the growth of nationalism in Europe."},
"Nationalism in India": {overview:"Indian nationalism grew through mass movements, shared symbols and resistance to colonial rule.",points:["The Rowlatt Act and Jallianwala Bagh intensified opposition. Gandhi developed satyagraha based on truth and non-violent resistance.","Non-Cooperation linked different groups, but their aims and experiences differed. Civil Disobedience began with the Salt March and expanded participation, including women and business groups.","The movement included diverse communities and did not remove all social and political differences. Symbols, songs, folklore and reinterpretations of history helped build collective identity."],practice:"Why did different social groups join the Civil Disobedience Movement?"},
"The Making of a Global World": {overview:"Globalisation has historical roots in trade, migration, conquest, technology and economic interdependence.",points:["Silk routes connected regions through goods, ideas and cultures long before modern globalisation.","The nineteenth-century world economy involved migration, capital flows and trade; colonialism shaped unequal relationships.","The Great Depression reduced trade and employment worldwide. Post-war institutions and later liberalisation changed patterns of global economic integration."],practice:"How did colonial rule affect trade and livelihoods in colonised regions?"},
"The Age of Industrialisation": {overview:"Industrialisation transformed production, labour, markets and everyday life unevenly across regions.",points:["Proto-industrial production used merchants and rural households before factory systems expanded.","Factories increased mechanised production, but hand labour and small producers continued where they remained flexible or met specialised demand.","In India, colonial policy, imported machine-made goods and changing markets affected weavers and industries; advertisements and labels helped establish brands."],practice:"Why did hand labour continue alongside machines in some industries?"},
"Print Culture and the Modern World": {overview:"Print technology widened access to ideas and helped transform religion, public debate and politics.",points:["Printing developed early in East Asia and spread to Europe; Gutenberg’s press expanded the production of books.","Print encouraged debate and reform but also prompted censorship and anxiety among authorities.","In India, printed material supported religious and social reform, new reading publics, nationalist ideas and debate over women’s education and caste."],practice:"Explain two ways print culture supported nationalism in India."},
"Resources and Development": {overview:"Resources become useful through human knowledge, technology and institutions; planning helps manage limited resources.",points:["Resources may be natural, human-made or human, and classified by origin, renewability, ownership and development status.","Resource planning includes identification and mapping, suitable planning structures and matching resource use with national plans.","Land degradation may result from deforestation, overgrazing, mining, over-irrigation or industrial waste. Soil conservation and sustainable use protect future needs."],practice:"Describe the main steps of resource planning."},
"Forest and Wildlife Resources": {overview:"Biodiversity supports ecosystems and livelihoods, while habitat loss and exploitation threaten species.",points:["Species can be classified by conservation status, including normal, vulnerable, endangered, rare, endemic and extinct.","Deforestation, habitat fragmentation, hunting, pollution and changing land use threaten biodiversity.","Conservation includes protected areas, community participation, habitat restoration and sustainable resource use. Local communities can be important stewards of forests."],practice:"Explain how local communities can help conserve forests and wildlife."},
"Water Resources": {overview:"Water availability varies by place and season; management must balance human use and ecological needs.",points:["Multipurpose river projects support irrigation, electricity and flood control but can displace people, alter ecosystems and cause disputes.","Rainwater harvesting captures runoff for local storage and groundwater recharge.","Integrated water management reduces wastage, protects catchments and promotes fair distribution. Traditional systems can offer locally adapted solutions."],practice:"Give two benefits and two concerns associated with large dams."},
"Agriculture": {overview:"Agriculture depends on climate, soil, water, technology, landholding patterns and markets.",points:["India has diverse farming types, including primitive subsistence, intensive subsistence and commercial farming.","Cropping seasons include rabi, kharif and zaid; crops differ in temperature, rainfall and soil requirements.","Institutional and technological reforms include irrigation, credit, crop insurance, improved seeds and market support, but sustainability and small-farmer security remain important."],practice:"Compare rabi and kharif crops with examples."},
"Minerals and Energy Resources": {overview:"Minerals and energy resources are unevenly distributed and must be used efficiently.",points:["Minerals occur in different geological formations; mining methods depend on depth, location and ore quality.","Conventional energy includes coal, petroleum, natural gas and electricity from established sources; non-conventional sources include solar, wind, tidal, geothermal and biogas.","Conservation, recycling and efficient technology reduce resource depletion and environmental damage."],practice:"Why is energy conservation important even when new energy sources are developed?"},
"Manufacturing Industries": {overview:"Manufacturing adds value to raw materials and supports employment, trade and economic growth.",points:["Industry location depends on raw materials, labour, capital, power, transport, markets and policy.","Agro-based industries use agricultural products; mineral-based industries use minerals. Industrial clusters may benefit from shared infrastructure.","Industrial pollution affects air, water, land and noise levels. Cleaner production, treatment plants, recycling and resource efficiency limit harm."],practice:"Explain how manufacturing industries contribute to economic development."},
"Lifelines of National Economy": {overview:"Transport, communication and trade connect people, markets and regions.",points:["Roadways offer flexible door-to-door service; railways move large volumes; pipelines carry liquids and gases; waterways and airways serve different distance and cost needs.","Communication networks enable information exchange and support economic services.","International trade connects economies; ports and airports function as gateways. Infrastructure choices affect accessibility and environmental impact."],practice:"Why are transport and communication called lifelines of an economy?"},
"Power Sharing": {overview:"Power sharing reduces the risk of domination and supports democratic stability.",points:["Belgium adopted arrangements sharing power among language communities; Sri Lanka’s majoritarian policies created alienation and conflict.","Power can be shared among organs of government, levels of government, social groups and political parties or pressure groups.","Power sharing is valuable for both prudential reasons (reducing conflict) and moral reasons (democratic participation)."],practice:"Explain horizontal and vertical power sharing with examples."},
"Federalism": {overview:"Federalism divides authority between levels of government, with each level having constitutionally assigned powers.",points:["A federal system has multiple levels of government, clear jurisdiction and constitutional protection for powers.","India’s Constitution divides subjects into Union, State and Concurrent Lists; residuary powers are with the Union.","Linguistic states, language policy and decentralisation have helped accommodate diversity. Local self-government expands participation at the grassroots."],practice:"State three key features of a federal system."},
"Gender, Religion and Caste": {overview:"Social divisions influence politics, while democratic institutions can address inequality and discrimination.",points:["Gender division is shaped by social expectations; political representation and equal opportunities remain important issues.","Secularism means the state should not favour one religion and should protect freedom of belief.","Caste inequalities can persist, but political expression can also help disadvantaged groups demand representation. No single identity fully determines political choices."],practice:"How can caste influence politics, and why is the relationship not one-way?"},
"Political Parties": {overview:"Political parties organise competition for power, form governments and connect citizens to public decisions.",points:["Parties contest elections, present policies, form and run governments, shape laws and act as opposition.","Challenges include lack of internal democracy, dynastic succession, money and muscle power, and limited meaningful choice.","Reforms may include transparency, internal elections, public pressure, citizen participation and stronger enforcement of rules."],practice:"Describe any three functions of political parties."},
"Outcomes of Democracy": {overview:"Democracy is judged by accountable government, responsiveness, legitimacy, development, equality and dignity.",points:["Democracy creates procedures for decision-making, but results can be slow because consultation and negotiation take time.","Democratic systems can manage social diversity and protect dignity and freedoms, though inequalities may remain.","Evaluate democracy using evidence and comparisons rather than assuming every outcome is automatically successful."],practice:"Why is democracy considered accountable and legitimate despite delays in decision-making?"},
"Development": {overview:"Development has different goals for different people and includes income as well as quality of life.",points:["People value income, security, equality, freedom, respect, public facilities and a healthy environment differently.","Per capita income is total income divided by population; it does not show how income is distributed.","Health, education, life expectancy and sustainability provide a broader picture of development than income alone."],practice:"Give two limitations of per capita income as a measure of development."},
"Sectors of the Indian Economy": {overview:"Economic activities are grouped by the type of work, ownership and employment conditions.",points:["Primary activities use natural resources; secondary activities manufacture; tertiary activities provide services.","GDP measures the value of final goods and services produced within a country during a period.","Formal employment generally offers more regulated conditions and benefits; informal workers often need social security and protection. Public and private sectors differ by ownership."],practice:"Distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary sectors with examples."},
"Money and Credit": {overview:"Money facilitates exchange, while credit can help production or create repayment difficulties.",points:["Money serves as a medium of exchange and removes the need for a double coincidence of wants.","Formal credit comes through regulated institutions; informal credit may carry higher interest and less protection.","Terms of credit include interest rate, collateral, documentation and repayment schedule. Self-help groups can improve access for members, especially women."],practice:"Why can cheap and accessible credit help development?"},
"Globalisation and the Indian Economy": {overview:"Globalisation connects production and markets across countries through trade, investment and technology.",points:["Multinational corporations spread or coordinate production across locations, choosing based on labour, markets, infrastructure and policy.","Foreign trade connects markets and increases competition and consumer choice, but benefits are not evenly distributed.","Fair globalisation seeks opportunities for workers and small producers as well as firms, supported by appropriate policies and standards."],practice:"How do multinational corporations organise production across countries?"},
"Consumer Rights": {overview:"Consumer rights protect people from unsafe goods, misleading claims and unfair trade practices.",points:["Consumers should receive safety, information, choice, representation, redressal and consumer education.","Check labels, prices, expiry dates, warranty terms and quality certification marks where applicable.","Keep bills and records when seeking redressal. Consumer awareness and enforcement both matter."],practice:"Why is a bill important when a consumer files a complaint?"}
},
"English": {
"A Letter to God": {overview:"A short story about Lencho’s faith, a ruined crop and the unexpected response to his letter.",points:["Lencho’s trust in God contrasts with the post office workers’ practical kindness.","Irony arises when Lencho calls the helpers crooks because the money is less than he expected.","Themes: faith, innocence, generosity and irony. Support answers with incidents from the text."],practice:"How does the story create irony at the end?"},
"Nelson Mandela: Long Walk to Freedom": {overview:"Mandela reflects on freedom, courage and the end of apartheid in South Africa.",points:["Apartheid denied rights on racial grounds; the inauguration marked a democratic beginning.","Courage is not the absence of fear but triumph over it. Oppression harms both the oppressed and the oppressor.","The text links personal freedom with collective freedom and responsibility."],practice:"What does Mandela say about courage and freedom?"},
"Two Stories about Flying": {overview:"The two stories explore fear, confidence and help during flight.",points:["In His First Flight, a young seagull overcomes fear when hunger drives him to fly.","In The Black Aeroplane, a pilot is guided through a storm by a mysterious aircraft; the ending remains puzzling.","Compare the different ways suspense and courage are developed."],practice:"How does the young seagull finally overcome his fear?"},
"From the Diary of Anne Frank": {overview:"Anne Frank explains why she begins writing a diary and reflects on school life and identity.",points:["Anne treats the diary as a trusted friend because she feels she needs someone to confide in.","Her essay about being talkative shows humour, self-awareness and how she responds to criticism.","Notice first-person narration and the contrast between her lively voice and wartime circumstances."],practice:"Why does Anne decide to keep a diary?"},
"Glimpses of India": {overview:"Three pieces describe distinct parts of India through traditions, landscapes and local life.",points:["A Baker from Goa recalls the role of the village baker and food traditions.","Coorg portrays a scenic region with distinctive culture and natural beauty; Tea from Assam explains tea landscapes and legends.","Use specific details to show how place, work and tradition shape regional identity."],practice:"What makes the baker important in Goan village life?"},
"Mijbil the Otter": {overview:"The narrator describes adopting an otter and learning about its curious behaviour.",points:["Mijbil’s playful habits create affection and gentle humour.","The story gives close observations of animal behaviour and the bond between human and pet.","Track the sequence of Mijbil’s adjustment to a new home and surroundings."],practice:"How does Mijbil’s behaviour reveal his personality?"},
"Madam Rides the Bus": {overview:"Valli plans a bus ride on her own, showing curiosity and independence.",points:["Valli carefully saves money and observes the route before taking the journey.","The sight of a dead cow changes her mood and introduces a mature awareness of death.","The story balances a child’s excitement with an emerging understanding of life."],practice:"What does Valli’s bus journey teach her?"},
"The Sermon at Benares": {overview:"Kisa Gotami learns that death is universal and grief is part of human life.",points:["The Buddha asks Kisa Gotami to find mustard seeds from a home untouched by death.","Her search reveals that every family experiences loss; she gradually accepts the truth.","Themes: impermanence, acceptance, compassion and the limits of personal sorrow."],practice:"How does the mustard-seed task help Kisa Gotami understand grief?"},
"The Proposal": {overview:"A comic one-act play in which a marriage proposal repeatedly turns into arguments.",points:["Lomov, Natalya and Chubukov are preoccupied with property, pride and petty disputes.","Exaggeration, interruptions and dramatic irony create humour.","The play satirises materialism and social expectations around marriage."],practice:"How does Chekhov use arguments to create comedy?"},
"Dust of Snow": {overview:"A small moment in nature changes the speaker’s mood.",points:["A crow and hemlock tree, often associated with negative ideas, cause a positive change.","The poem shows that even a brief, unexpected event can improve a difficult day.","Notice imagery, symbolism and the contrast between mood before and after."],practice:"How do the crow and hemlock tree contribute to the poem’s message?"},
"Fire and Ice": {overview:"The poem compares destructive human emotions to fire and ice.",points:["Fire symbolises desire; ice symbolises hatred or cold indifference.","Both can destroy, suggesting different emotions can lead to similar consequences.","The compact structure and contrasting images create a warning about human behaviour."],practice:"What do fire and ice symbolise?"},
"A Tiger in the Zoo": {overview:"The poem contrasts a tiger’s confinement with the freedom it should have in the wild.",points:["The caged tiger paces silently, showing frustration and lost freedom.","The imagined wild tiger would stalk through the forest and assert its natural power.","Contrast and imagery highlight captivity and the importance of habitat."],practice:"Contrast the tiger in the zoo with the tiger in the wild."},
"How to Tell Wild Animals": {overview:"A humorous poem gives exaggerated ways of identifying wild animals.",points:["Irony and playful exaggeration make the descriptions comic rather than scientific instructions.","Learn the distinguishing descriptions of the animals named in the poem.","The humour relies on understatement and deliberately impractical advice."],practice:"How does the poet create humour in the descriptions?"},
"The Ball Poem": {overview:"A boy loses a ball and begins to learn about loss and responsibility.",points:["The ball represents more than a toy; it carries emotional value and memories.","The poet suggests that people must learn to accept loss as part of growing up.","The speaker does not simply replace the ball because the experience itself matters."],practice:"Why is the loss of the ball important for the boy?"},
"Amanda!": {overview:"The poem contrasts repeated instructions from an adult with Amanda’s imagined worlds.",points:["Amanda imagines herself as a mermaid, an orphan and Rapunzel to escape constant correction.","Repetition and contrasting voices show the tension between discipline and a child’s need for space.","The poem invites reflection on how adults communicate expectations."],practice:"Why does Amanda escape into imagination?"},
"The Trees": {overview:"The poem imagines trees moving out of a house and returning to the forest.",points:["The trees can be read as a symbol of nature seeking freedom from confinement.","Personification and imagery make the movement feel purposeful and inevitable.","The poem contrasts artificial indoor space with the natural world."],practice:"What do the trees symbolise in the poem?"},
"Fog": {overview:"Fog arrives and leaves quietly, compared to a cat.",points:["The cat simile captures the fog’s silent, watchful movement.","Short lines reflect the brief, quiet appearance of fog.","Focus on imagery, simile and the mood created with very few words."],practice:"Why is fog compared to a cat?"},
"The Tale of Custard the Dragon": {overview:"A playful ballad contrasts claims of bravery with real courage.",points:["Belinda’s pets boast of bravery, while Custard asks for a safe cage.","When danger arrives, Custard acts bravely while the others flee.","The poem uses rhyme, repetition, humour and a twist to question boastfulness."],practice:"How does Custard prove to be the bravest character?"},
"For Anne Gregory": {overview:"The poem questions whether people love inner qualities or outward appearance.",points:["The discussion of golden hair represents physical beauty and how it can shape attraction.","The poem contrasts human preference for appearance with a deeper, spiritual kind of love.","Explain the speaker’s message using the final exchange."],practice:"What does the poem suggest about love and physical beauty?"},
"A Triumph of Surgery": {overview:"A pampered dog’s health improves when excessive indulgence is replaced with routine and exercise.",points:["Mrs Pumphrey’s affection results in overfeeding and inactivity for Tricki.","Dr Herriot uses diet, exercise and a simple routine instead of unnecessary treatment.","The story uses humour to show that care needs balance and practical judgment."],practice:"How does Dr Herriot help Tricki recover?"},
"The Thief’s Story": {overview:"A young thief begins to change after experiencing trust and kindness.",points:["Hari Singh expects to exploit Anil but is affected by Anil’s trust and simple generosity.","Returning the money marks a moral turning point and a desire to learn.","The story suggests education, trust and second chances can encourage change."],practice:"Why does Hari Singh return to Anil?"},
"The Midnight Visitor": {overview:"A detective uses calm thinking and a made-up balcony to outwit an armed intruder.",points:["Ausable does not match the stereotypical image of a spy, but relies on intelligence.","He invents a story about a balcony and uses a knock at the door to create urgency.","The story builds suspense through dialogue and a clever reversal."],practice:"How does Ausable defeat Max without using physical force?"},
"A Question of Trust": {overview:"A careful burglar is deceived by a woman who appears to be the owner of a house.",points:["Horace Danby plans robberies carefully but assumes the woman is trustworthy.","The woman tricks him into opening the safe, and he is later arrested.","Irony arises because the criminal becomes the victim of another criminal’s deception."],practice:"Why does Horace Danby fall for the woman’s story?"},
"Footprints without Feet": {overview:"A scientist discovers invisibility but uses it irresponsibly.",points:["Griffin’s scientific discovery does not make him ethical or wise.","His thefts and aggression bring trouble, showing that scientific power without responsibility is dangerous.","The title refers to the visible evidence left by an otherwise invisible person."],practice:"How does Griffin misuse his scientific discovery?"},
"The Making of a Scientist": {overview:"Richard Ebright’s curiosity, observation and persistence help him become a scientist.",points:["His mother supports his interests and provides books, tools and opportunities.","Science fair experiments teach him to ask clear questions and design meaningful investigations.","Curiosity, disciplined work and learning from failure matter more than collecting prizes alone."],practice:"What qualities helped Richard Ebright become a scientist?"},
"The Necklace": {overview:"Mathilde’s dissatisfaction with her life leads to a costly mistake and years of hardship.",points:["Mathilde values social status and appearance, and borrows a necklace to attend a ball.","She and her husband replace the lost necklace at great cost, only to learn the original was imitation jewellery.","Situational irony and the theme of appearance versus reality drive the story."],practice:"Explain the irony in the ending of The Necklace."},
"Bholi": {overview:"Education and a supportive teacher help Bholi gain confidence and resist an unfair marriage.",points:["Bholi is mocked for her appearance and stammer, which affects her self-esteem.","Her teacher’s patience encourages her to speak and learn.","Bholi refuses to marry Bishamber after he demands dowry, showing self-respect and independence."],practice:"How does education transform Bholi?"},
"The Book That Saved the Earth": {overview:"A comic science-fiction play shows how an ordinary nursery rhyme book stops a Martian invasion.",points:["Think-Tank’s arrogance leads him to misinterpret a book and Earth’s culture.","Noodle’s more careful suggestions contrast with Think-Tank’s overconfidence.","The play uses satire and dramatic irony to show the dangers of poor reasoning and unchecked pride."],practice:"How does the book save Earth from the Martians?"}
},
"Hindi A": {
"सूरदास के पद": {overview:"इन पदों में कृष्ण के बालरूप, गोपियों के प्रेम और भक्ति-भाव का चित्रण मिलता है।",points:["कवि ने वात्सल्य और भक्ति रस के माध्यम से कृष्ण की मनोहर छवि प्रस्तुत की है।","भाषा में ब्रजभाषा, लय, संवाद और भावपूर्ण पदावली का प्रयोग मिलता है।","उत्तर में प्रसंग, भावार्थ और काव्य-सौंदर्य को अलग-अलग स्पष्ट करें।"],practice:"पदों में व्यक्त वात्सल्य और भक्ति भाव उदाहरण सहित समझाइए।"},
"राम-लक्ष्मण-परशुराम संवाद": {overview:"यह प्रसंग धनुष टूटने के बाद राम, लक्ष्मण और परशुराम के बीच संवाद और स्वभावों का अंतर दिखाता है।",points:["लक्ष्मण के व्यंग्यपूर्ण उत्तर और परशुराम के क्रोध से नाटकीयता उत्पन्न होती है।","राम विनम्रता, संयम और मर्यादा का परिचय देते हैं।","तुलसीदास की भाषा में अवधी, संवाद-शैली, वीर रस और हास्य-व्यंग्य का प्रभाव है।"],practice:"राम और लक्ष्मण के स्वभाव में अंतर स्पष्ट कीजिए।"},
"साखी": {overview:"कबीर की साखियाँ जीवन, आत्मज्ञान, विनम्रता और सच्चे आचरण का संदेश देती हैं।",points:["कबीर बाहरी आडंबर की अपेक्षा मन की शुद्धता और अनुभव को महत्व देते हैं।","साखी दोहे जैसी संक्षिप्त शैली में गहरा अर्थ व्यक्त करती है।","उत्तर में प्रतीक, संदेश और आज के जीवन से संबंध लिखें।"],practice:"कबीर की साखियों का मुख्य संदेश अपने शब्दों में लिखिए।"},
"पद": {overview:"इन पदों में भक्ति, विरह या ईश्वर के प्रति समर्पण जैसे भावों को काव्यात्मक ढंग से व्यक्त किया गया है।",points:["काव्य में भाव, लय और चुने हुए शब्द अनुभूति को प्रभावी बनाते हैं।","कविता के वक्ता, संबोधित व्यक्ति और मूल भाव को पहचानें।","भावार्थ लिखते समय पंक्ति का अर्थ सरल भाषा में दें, केवल शब्द-दर-शब्द अनुवाद न करें।"],practice:"कविता के मुख्य भाव और भाषा-सौंदर्य को समझाइए।"},
"मनुष्यता": {overview:"कविता सच्चे मनुष्य की पहचान परोपकार, करुणा और दूसरों के लिए जीने में मानती है।",points:["मानवता का अर्थ केवल अपना सुख नहीं, बल्कि दूसरों के दुःख को समझना भी है।","त्याग, सहानुभूति और सहयोग कविता के प्रमुख मूल्य हैं।","उत्तर में कविता के संदेश को किसी उपयुक्त उदाहरण से जोड़ें।"],practice:"कविता के अनुसार सच्ची मनुष्यता क्या है?"},
"पर्वत प्रदेश में पावस": {overview:"कविता वर्षा ऋतु में पर्वतीय प्रकृति के बदलते रूपों का सजीव चित्र बनाती है।",points:["बादल, झरने, ताल और पर्वत के चित्रों से दृश्यात्मकता पैदा होती है।","मानवीकरण और उपमा प्रकृति को जीवंत बनाते हैं।","कविता के वातावरण, दृश्य और ध्वनि-सौंदर्य को पहचानें।"],practice:"वर्षा में पर्वतीय प्रदेश के बदलते दृश्य का वर्णन कीजिए।"},
"तोप": {overview:"कविता पुरानी तोप के माध्यम से सत्ता, हिंसा और समय के बदलाव पर व्यंग्य करती है।",points:["तोप कभी शक्ति का प्रतीक थी, पर अब इतिहास की वस्तु बन गई है।","बच्चों और चिड़ियों का उसके पास सहज रहना सत्ता की क्षणभंगुरता दर्शाता है।","कविता के प्रतीकात्मक अर्थ और व्यंग्य को स्पष्ट करें।"],practice:"तोप कविता में समय के बदलाव का संकेत कैसे देती है?"},
"कर चले हम फ़िदा": {overview:"यह देशभक्ति कविता सैनिकों के बलिदान और राष्ट्र की रक्षा का संदेश देती है।",points:["कविता में त्याग, साहस, कर्तव्य और देशप्रेम मुख्य भाव हैं।","सैनिक अगली पीढ़ी को देश की जिम्मेदारी निभाने का आह्वान करते हैं।","उत्तर में भावात्मकता और प्रेरक स्वर पर ध्यान दें।"],practice:"कविता देशवासियों को क्या संदेश देती है?"},
"आत्मत्राण": {overview:"कविता कठिनाइयों से बचाने की बजाय उनका सामना करने का साहस माँगने की भावना व्यक्त करती है।",points:["कवि आत्मबल, धैर्य और निर्भयता को महत्व देता है।","आस्था का अर्थ केवल संकट हटाने की प्रार्थना नहीं, बल्कि उससे लड़ने की शक्ति पाना है।","कविता का संदेश आत्मनिर्भरता और मानसिक दृढ़ता से जुड़ा है।"],practice:"कवि किस प्रकार की सहायता चाहता है और क्यों?"},
"नेताजी का चश्मा": {overview:"कहानी देशभक्ति, सम्मान और साधारण लोगों की संवेदना को दिखाती है।",points:["कैप्टन चश्मेवाला नेताजी के प्रति अपनी श्रद्धा साधारण तरीके से व्यक्त करता है।","हवलदार और हालदार साहब की प्रतिक्रियाएँ समाज की सोच और भावनाओं को सामने लाती हैं।","अंत में बच्चे का प्रयास आशा और देशप्रेम के बने रहने का संकेत है।"],practice:"कहानी के अंत का क्या महत्व है?"},
"बालगोबिन भगत": {overview:"रेखाचित्र में बालगोबिन भगत का सरल जीवन, भक्ति, श्रम और सामाजिक रूढ़ियों से अलग दृष्टिकोण चित्रित है।",points:["वे कबीर के पद गाते हुए श्रम और भक्ति को साथ रखते हैं।","पुत्र की मृत्यु पर उनका व्यवहार जीवन-दर्शन और आत्मिक विश्वास को दर्शाता है।","लेखक ने चरित्र-चित्रण के लिए व्यवहार, वाणी और जीवन-शैली का उपयोग किया है।"],practice:"बालगोबिन भगत के चरित्र की प्रमुख विशेषताएँ लिखिए।"},
"लखनवी अंदाज़": {overview:"यह व्यंग्य नवाबी दिखावे और बनावटी शिष्टाचार पर हास्यपूर्ण टिप्पणी करता है।",points:["खीरों के प्रसंग से व्यवहार की कृत्रिमता और दिखावा उजागर होता है।","लेखक की दृष्टि में बाहरी तहज़ीब और वास्तविक सहजता में अंतर है।","व्यंग्य को समझने के लिए कथन और उसके निहित अर्थ पर ध्यान दें।"],practice:"लेखक ने लखनवी अंदाज़ पर व्यंग्य कैसे किया है?"},
"मानवीय करुणा की दिव्य चमक": {overview:"पाठ फादर कामिल बुल्के के जीवन में विद्वता, सेवा, करुणा और मानवीय संवेदना को रेखांकित करता है।",points:["उनकी भाषा-सेवा, साहित्यिक कार्य और मानवीय व्यवहार उनके व्यक्तित्व के मुख्य पक्ष हैं।","करुणा को दैनिक व्यवहार और दूसरों के साथ संबंधों में देखा जा सकता है।","चरित्र-चित्रण में गुणों के साथ पाठ से प्रमाण भी लिखें।"],practice:"फादर बुल्के के व्यक्तित्व में मानवीय करुणा कैसे दिखाई देती है?"},
"एक कहानी यह भी": {overview:"यह आत्मकथात्मक अंश लेखिका के बचपन, परिवार, शिक्षा और व्यक्तित्व-निर्माण का अनुभव बताता है।",points:["परिवार और सामाजिक वातावरण व्यक्ति की सोच पर प्रभाव डालते हैं।","लेखिका अपने अनुभवों के माध्यम से स्वतंत्र विचार और आत्मपहचान की यात्रा दिखाती हैं।","उत्तर में घटनाओं को क्रम से लिखें और उनके प्रभाव की व्याख्या करें।"],practice:"लेखिका के व्यक्तित्व-निर्माण में परिवार और अनुभवों की भूमिका बताइए।"},
"स्त्री शिक्षा के विरोधी कुतर्कों का खंडन": {overview:"पाठ स्त्री-शिक्षा के विरोध में दिए गए तर्कों की आलोचना करता है और शिक्षा की आवश्यकता बताता है।",points:["शिक्षा व्यक्ति को विवेक, स्वतंत्रता और समाज में भागीदारी देती है।","लेखक रूढ़ धारणाओं को प्रश्नों और तर्कों से चुनौती देता है।","निबंध के उत्तर में तर्क, उदाहरण और निष्कर्ष व्यवस्थित ढंग से प्रस्तुत करें।"],practice:"लेखक स्त्री-शिक्षा के विरोधी तर्कों का खंडन कैसे करता है?"},
"नौबतखाने में इबादत": {overview:"पाठ उस्ताद बिस्मिल्ला खाँ के संगीत-साधना, विनम्रता और सांस्कृतिक एकता को दर्शाता है।",points:["शहनाई की साधना में निरंतर अभ्यास, गुरु-परंपरा और समर्पण का महत्व है।","बिस्मिल्ला खाँ का जीवन संगीत और सांस्कृतिक समन्वय का उदाहरण है।","स्थान, परंपरा और संगीत के संबंध को पाठ के प्रसंगों से समझें।"],practice:"बिस्मिल्ला खाँ की साधना और व्यक्तित्व की विशेषताएँ लिखिए।"},
"संस्कृति": {overview:"निबंध संस्कृति और सभ्यता के संबंध तथा मानव के ज्ञान, व्यवहार और मूल्यों पर विचार करता है।",points:["सभ्यता बाहरी साधनों और व्यवस्थाओं से जुड़ी है; संस्कृति में विचार, मूल्य और जीवन-दृष्टि भी शामिल होते हैं।","ज्ञान का उपयोग मानव कल्याण के लिए होना चाहिए।","लेखक के विचारों को अपने शब्दों में स्पष्ट उदाहरण के साथ लिखें।"],practice:"संस्कृति और सभ्यता में अंतर स्पष्ट कीजिए।"},
"माता का आँचल": {overview:"पाठ ग्रामीण बचपन, परिवार और माँ-पिता के स्नेहपूर्ण संबंधों की स्मृतियाँ प्रस्तुत करता है।",points:["बालक की दुनिया में खेल, भय, अपनापन और घरेलू जीवन महत्वपूर्ण हैं।","माँ का आँचल सुरक्षा और भावनात्मक सहारे का प्रतीक है।","आत्मकथात्मक शैली, ग्रामीण वातावरण और बाल-मन के चित्रण पर ध्यान दें।"],practice:"माता का आँचल शीर्षक की सार्थकता बताइए।"},
"जॉर्ज पंचम की नाक": {overview:"व्यंग्य औपनिवेशिक मानसिकता और सत्ता के सामने झुकने वाली व्यवस्था की आलोचना करता है।",points:["प्रतिमा की नाक का प्रसंग प्रतीकात्मक रूप से शासक के सम्मान और प्रशासनिक दिखावे को दिखाता है।","हास्य और अतिशयोक्ति से अधिकारियों की प्राथमिकताएँ उजागर होती हैं।","उत्तर में व्यंग्य का लक्ष्य और लेखक का संदेश स्पष्ट करें।"],practice:"जॉर्ज पंचम की नाक कहानी में किस मानसिकता पर व्यंग्य है?"},
"एही ठैयाँ झुलनी हेरानी हो रामा!": {overview:"पाठ लोक-संस्कृति, संगीत और बदलते सामाजिक परिवेश के बीच कलाकारों के जीवन को सामने लाता है।",points:["लोकगीत और संगीत सामुदायिक स्मृति तथा पहचान के वाहक होते हैं।","पाठ के पात्रों के संघर्ष को ऐतिहासिक और सामाजिक संदर्भ में समझें।","उत्तर में लोक-संस्कृति के महत्व और पाठ के मानवीय पक्ष को जोड़ें।"],practice:"पाठ में लोक-संस्कृति और कलाकारों की स्थिति का चित्रण समझाइए।"},
"मैं क्यों लिखता हूँ": {overview:"लेखक लेखन के पीछे की प्रेरणा, अनुभव और आंतरिक आवश्यकता पर विचार करता है।",points:["लेखन केवल बाहरी दबाव नहीं, बल्कि अनुभवों और विचारों को व्यक्त करने की आंतरिक आवश्यकता भी है।","लेखक अपने अनुभवों से सृजन-प्रक्रिया को समझाता है।","मुख्य विचार, उदाहरण और लेखक का निष्कर्ष अलग-अलग पहचानें।"],practice:"लेखक के अनुसार लिखने की प्रेरणा कहाँ से आती है?"}
},
"AI (417)": {
"Communication Skills-II": {overview:"Effective communication involves clear speaking, attentive listening, suitable body language and useful feedback.",points:["Use simple language, appropriate tone and active listening; avoid interrupting or assuming meaning.","Non-verbal communication includes posture, facial expressions, gestures and eye contact, interpreted in context.","The communication cycle includes sender, message, channel, receiver and feedback. Barriers can be physical, linguistic or emotional."],practice:"Give two examples of communication barriers and how to reduce them."},
"Self-management Skills-II": {overview:"Self-management means planning work, regulating emotions and building healthy habits.",points:["Set specific and realistic goals, prioritise tasks and review progress regularly.","Stress management can include breathing, activity, sleep, problem-solving and talking with a trusted person.","Strengths, interests and areas for improvement help create a personal development plan."],practice:"Write a realistic goal and break it into three actions."},
"ICT Skills-II": {overview:"ICT skills support safe, organised and effective use of digital devices and information.",points:["Organise files with meaningful names and folders; keep backups of important work.","Use strong unique passwords, updates and trusted sources; beware of phishing and suspicious attachments.","Respect privacy, copyright, digital etiquette and the security of other people's data."],practice:"List three practices that help keep an online account secure."},
"Entrepreneurial Skills-II": {overview:"Entrepreneurship identifies needs and creates value while managing risk and resources.",points:["Entrepreneurs notice problems, develop solutions and consider customers, costs and feasibility.","Common qualities include initiative, resilience, creativity, communication and responsible decision-making.","A basic business plan identifies the product, target customer, resources, costs, price and way to reach customers."],practice:"Choose a local problem and outline a simple product or service that could address it."},
"Green Skills-II": {overview:"Green skills help reduce environmental harm and support sustainable ways of living and working.",points:["Sustainable development meets current needs without compromising future generations' ability to meet theirs.","Reduce, reuse, repair and recycle; conserve water and energy and minimise waste.","Green jobs and practices appear in renewable energy, efficient buildings, sustainable agriculture, transport and waste management."],practice:"Suggest three practical ways a school can reduce its environmental footprint."},
"Introduction to AI": {overview:"Artificial intelligence refers to systems designed to perform tasks that usually require human-like capabilities such as recognising patterns or understanding language.",points:["AI examples include recommendation systems, image recognition, translation and voice assistants.","AI, machine learning and deep learning are related terms: machine learning learns patterns from data; deep learning uses multi-layer neural networks.","AI output depends on data and design. Human oversight is important when decisions affect people."],practice:"Give two everyday examples of AI and explain what each does."},
"AI Project Cycle": {overview:"A typical AI project cycle organises work from defining a problem through data, modelling and evaluation.",points:["Problem scoping clarifies the problem, goals, stakeholders and constraints.","Data acquisition gathers relevant and lawful data; data exploration checks quality, patterns, missing values and possible bias.","Modelling trains or configures a solution; evaluation checks performance against appropriate criteria. A project may need iterations."],practice:"Describe the stages of an AI project cycle in order."},
"Advanced Python": {overview:"Python programming builds solutions using variables, conditions, loops, collections and reusable functions.",points:["Use meaningful identifiers, correct indentation and suitable data types; strings, lists and dictionaries serve different purposes.","Conditions choose a path; loops repeat work; functions organise reusable steps and may accept arguments and return values.","Test normal, boundary and invalid inputs. Read error messages, trace variable values and break complex problems into smaller functions."],practice:"Write pseudocode to find the largest of three numbers."},
"Data Science": {overview:"Data science explores data to answer questions and communicate evidence.",points:["A workflow includes asking a question, collecting data, cleaning it, exploring patterns, analysing, visualising and communicating findings.","Mean is sensitive to outliers; median is more robust for skewed data; mode identifies the most frequent value.","Correlation does not by itself prove causation. Check sample size, missing data, bias, units and the source before drawing conclusions."],practice:"When might median be a better summary than mean?"},
"Computer Vision": {overview:"Computer vision systems analyse images or video to identify patterns such as objects, faces or features.",points:["Images are represented as pixels; colour images often use channels such as red, green and blue.","Tasks include image classification, object detection and segmentation. Data quality, lighting, angle and occlusion affect results.","Evaluate accuracy and errors across different groups and settings. Obtain consent and protect privacy when working with people's images."],practice:"Explain the difference between image classification and object detection."},
"Natural Language Processing": {overview:"NLP enables computers to process, analyse or generate human language.",points:["Tasks include sentiment analysis, translation, text classification, summarisation and chat interfaces.","Pre-processing can include tokenisation and normalisation, but choices depend on the task and language.","Language is ambiguous and culturally varied. NLP models can reflect bias, hallucinate or misinterpret context, so outputs need checking."],practice:"Give two NLP applications and one limitation."},
"Evaluation": {overview:"Evaluation checks whether an AI model meets its intended goal on data it has not simply memorised.",points:["Separate training and test data; use validation data when tuning models.","Accuracy is the share of correct predictions. For classification, precision measures how many predicted positives are correct; recall measures how many actual positives are found.","Choose metrics based on consequences of false positives and false negatives. Compare results with a baseline and document limitations."],practice:"Why should test data not be used to tune a model repeatedly?"},
"AI Ethics and Responsible AI": {overview:"Responsible AI aims to make systems fair, accountable, transparent, safe and respectful of privacy.",points:["Potential concerns include bias, privacy loss, lack of transparency, unsafe decisions and unequal access.","Use only necessary data with proper permission; protect it and explain important decisions where possible.","Keep humans accountable, test systems in varied situations and provide ways to question or correct harmful outcomes."],practice:"Identify two ethical risks in facial recognition and suggest safeguards."}
}
};